module

public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Dimension.Finrank
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.FiniteDimensional.Defs
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Reflection
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Subgroup.Lattice

/-!
# Roots in a simple-root subspace are subsystem Weyl translates

Source: Milne, *Algebraic Groups*, Appendix C.12--C.22, printed pp.610--612,
especially C.22, applied to the root system in the span of a subset of a base
(blueprint: lem-sfb-root-subsystem-simple-orbits).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

namespace RootPairing

/-- The roots in the rational span of a subset of a simple base are exactly its integral
root span and its orbits under the subgroup generated by its simple reflections.
Source: Milne, *Algebraic Groups*, Appendix C.12--C.22, printed pp.610--612,
especially C.22 (blueprint: lem-sfb-root-subsystem-simple-orbits).

Proof (the eight elementary steps of the node):
1. Each reflection is involutive and sends its defining root to its negative. The group
generated by all reflections embeds in the permutation group of the finite spanning root
set, and is finite. If `I` is empty, all three root sets are empty, including when `R` is empty.
2. In the given base, the sum of products of rational coordinates is positive definite.
Summing its translates over the finite group gives an invariant positive definite form `B`.
3. Reflection invariance and the negative image of the defining root give
`c a x = 2 * B x a / B a a`, so positivity of `B x a` gives positivity of `c a x`.
4. Uniqueness of base coordinates identifies rational-span membership with vanishing
coordinates outside `I`. Integral root coordinates then give the integral-span equality.
Positive roots have positive integer height, the sum of their coordinates.
5. If a positive root `r` supported on `I` is not simple, expanding `B r r` gives a simple
root `a` in `I` with positive coordinate and `B r a > 0`. The integer `m = c a r` is positive.
Reducedness forces another positive coordinate at a simple root different from `a`.
6. Reflecting gives `r - m • a`, still a root supported on `I`. The other positive coordinate
is unchanged, so sign coherence makes this root positive; its height decreases by `m`.
7. Strong induction on height now puts every positive supported root in an orbit of a
simple root in `I`. Height one is simple. For a negative root, apply the positive case to
its negative and compose with the reflection at the resulting simple root.
8. Conversely, each generating reflection preserves the root set and the rational span of
`I`, as does its inverse, which is itself. Thus the generated subgroup gives only roots in
that span. Membership in the generated subgroup means a finite word in these involutions,
so this also gives the asserted finite product of simple reflections.
-/
theorem root_subsystem_simple_orbits
    (V : Type u) [AddCommGroup V] [Module ℚ V] [FiniteDimensional ℚ V]
    (R : Set V) (hRfinite : R.Finite) (hRzero : (0 : V) ∉ R)
    (hRspan : Submodule.span ℚ R = ⊤)
    (c : R → V →ₗ[ℚ] ℚ) (hc2 : ∀ a : R, c a a.1 = 2)
    (hcint : ∀ a r : R, ∃ z : ℤ, c a r.1 = (z : ℚ))
    (hcperm : ∀ a : R, Set.BijOn (Module.reflection (hc2 a)) R R)
    (hRline : ∀ a : R,
      R ∩ (Submodule.span ℚ {a.1} : Set V) = {a.1, -a.1})
    (Δ : Set V) (hΔ : Δ ⊆ R) (b : Module.Basis Δ ℚ V)
    (hb : ∀ a : Δ, b a = a.1)
    (hcoords : ∀ r : R, ∃ n : Δ → ℤ,
      (∀ a : Δ, b.repr r.1 a = (n a : ℚ)) ∧
      ((∀ a : Δ, 0 ≤ n a) ∨ (∀ a : Δ, n a ≤ 0)))
    (I : Set V) (hI : I ⊆ Δ) :
    R ∩ (Submodule.span ℚ I : Set V) = R ∩ (AddSubgroup.closure I : Set V) ∧
      R ∩ (AddSubgroup.closure I : Set V) =
        {x : V | ∃ w : V ≃ₗ[ℚ] V,
          w ∈ Subgroup.closure
            {s : V ≃ₗ[ℚ] V | ∃ a : R, a.1 ∈ I ∧ s = Module.reflection (hc2 a)} ∧
          ∃ a ∈ I, w a = x} := by
  sorry

end RootPairing
